From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 08:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a733: dp: DisplayPort audio through DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DP_AUDIO

WHAT
The A733 DisplayPort transmitter (Trilinear DP 1.4, 0x05740000) has an audio
half, the SEC0 block, UM 15.4.7.65-87 (0x0900-0x0958). This patch drives it from
the bridge: DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DP_AUDIO with dp_audio_startup / prepare / shutdown /
mute_stream, hdmi_audio_dev = the DP device, 8 I2S channels, dai_port -1.
drm_bridge_connector then registers an hdmi-codec under the DP node
(drm_hdmi_audio_helper.c:184) and a simple-audio-card reaches it as
`sound-dai = <&edp0>` (the component's of_node is its parent's,
soc-core.c:827-835). The CPU side is I2S3 (UM ch17 assigns it to HDMI or eDP).

WHERE EVERY VALUE COMES FROM
Sequence (15.4.7.65: control registers set first, enable last):
  0x0900 AUDIO_ENABLE        <- 0x2 (mute, packets off; UM reset value; also the
                                only clear of FIFO overflow, 15.4.7.87)
  0x0904 AUDIO_INPUT_SELECT  <- 0, "00 = I2S" (15.4.7.66)
  0x0908 CHANNEL_COUNT       <- 0x2 / 0x3 (2ch+LFE) / 0x6 / 0x8 (15.4.7.67);
                                other counts are refused: the UM lists no code
  0x0920 AUDIO_CLOCK_MODE    <- 0, asynchronous (15.4.7.73); MAUD/NAUD (0x0918,
                                0x091C) only matter in synchronous mode
                                (15.4.7.71/72) and are not written -> no rate
                                table, no link-rate dependency
  0x0954 AUDIO_CHANNEL_MAP   <- 0x76543210 identity (15.4.7.86, values 0-7)
  0x0940-0x094C channel status <- fields of hparms->iec.status, bit positions
                                named by the UM (15.4.7.81-84), byte layout from
                                include/sound/asoundef.h
  0x0934/0x0938/0x0914/0x0910 audio InfoFrame via
                                hdmi_audio_infoframe_pack_for_dp(), version 0x11
                                (mtk_dp.c:52, msm/dp/dp_audio.c:68)
  0x0900 AUDIO_ENABLE        <- 0x3 (packets on, still muted)
  .mute_stream: 0x1 / 0x3 (AUDIO_MUTE = VBID mute bit, 15.4.7.65)
  .shutdown: 0x2, InfoFrame enable bit cleared.
Callback contract and bridge fields: include/drm/drm_bridge.h:812-870,
drm_bridge_connector.c:455-549, 718-732, 797-815; same wiring as
msm/dp/dp_drm.c:329-335 and rockchip/cdn-dp-core.c:1003-1009.
zynqmp_dp_audio.c / zynqmp_dp.c:1383-1415 (register-similar core) is an ASoC
DAI with its own DMA, not a bridge: only its order (channels, clocks, one
enable write last) is borrowed.
Presence check: CORE_CAPABILITIES (0x00F8) AUDIO_PRESENT bit 9 (UM 15.4.7).
Kconfig: select DRM_DISPLAY_HDMI_AUDIO_HELPER, imply SND_SOC_HDMI_CODEC
(imply, not select: this driver is =y, SND_SOC is =m on the product kernel).

WHAT THIS COVERS THAT THE VENDOR DRIVER DOES NOT, AND WHERE IT DIFFERS
(trilinear_dp14.c:1009-1100, sunxi_drm_edp.c:4918-4988)
 - Vendor runs synchronous mode with a MAUD/NAUD table for 1.62 and 2.7 Gb/s.
   The UM offers asynchronous mode; this takes it. The vendor table also has a
   row labelled 90600 Hz whose M/N are those of 96 kHz.
 - Vendor audio_disable() sets bit 1 (the MUTE bit) of AUDIO_ENABLE and never
   clears the packet-enable bit 0: "disable" is a mute.
 - Vendor writes CHANNEL_COUNT 0x3 for stereo ("fix some displayport panel no
   audio ouput issue") although 0x3 is 2ch+LFE in the UM and 0x2 is stereo; it
   maps 5 channels to 0x6, any other count to 0x8. Not ported: this takes the
   UM codes and refuses counts the UM has no code for.
 - Vendor never writes the audio InfoFrame, channel status or channel map.
 - Vendor supports SPDIF input (source 3 = "OWA, future enhancement" in the UM);
   not ported, I2S only.
 - The I2S3/I2S4 -> eDP source select is NOT ported (see below).

VENDOR-ONLY, TO BE READ BACK ON SILICON
Nothing in the driver is vendor-only. The one vendor-only fact is deliberately
not in code: SYSCFG 0x0300023C bit 0 (EDP_I2S_SEL, snd_sun60iw2_syscfg.c:30-47)
with enum text {"I2S3","I2S4"}, i.e. 0 = I2S3, 1 = I2S4. The UM has no such
register (grep of the whole UM text: nothing). Not implemented because I2S3 is
the path this patch wires and, if the reset value is 0, the default already
selects it. If `devmem 0x0300023C` reads bit 0 = 1 at boot, I2S3 audio will
not arrive: that is the trigger to add a syscon property and one
regmap_update_bits(); until then a write would only be an unproven guess.
Needed for I2S4 (2-lane, max 4 channels) only.

VERIFIED
Applies on the 6.18.54 series and builds warning-free:
  make drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/ allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dtb W=1
Nothing else. Never run on hardware; the bridge callbacks have never been
reached, because no hdmi-codec is registered without a KMS driver.

OWED ON SILICON
 1. CORE_CAPABILITIES (0x057400F8) bit 9 reads 1 with the block enabled (UM
    default 0x01441f04).
 2. `devmem 0x0300023C` bit 0 == 0 (I2S3 selected): READ 2026-10-02 on
    the 8 GiB board, our 6.18.54 kernel, no sink: 0x00000000, so the reset state selects
    I2S3 and no write is needed for this path.
 3. With a sink that has speakers: EDID CEA audio block / drm connector ELD
    non-empty (/proc/asound/cardN/eld#0.0), then `speaker-test -c2 -r48000`.
    After prepare: 0x0900 reads 0x3, 0x0904 0, 0x0908 2, 0x0920 0, 0x0954
    0x76543210; after unmute 0x0900 = 0x1; 0x0958 stays 0 (overflow);
    0x095C SEC0_PACKET_COUNT counts up.
 4. Asynchronous clock mode actually produces sound. If not:
    devmem 0x05740918 32 512; devmem 0x0574091c 32 5625; devmem 0x05740920 32 1
    (48 kHz at 2.7 Gb/s, DP 1.4 2.2.5.2.2; 44.1 kHz = 784/9375).
 5. The audio InfoFrame byte layout: the UM does not say whether HB0-HB3 are part
    of the bytes written to SDP_BUFFER_DATA, nor the slot length; slot 0 is this
    driver's choice. Check on the sink (e.g. a DP analyzer or the monitor's
    speaker allocation); the audio stream itself does not depend on it.
 6. Channel map: identity written; if L/R arrive swapped or all on one speaker,
    the reset value 0 was a bypass and the write is the culprit.
 7. SEC0_CS_COPYRIGHT / CS_USER_DATA left at 0 (polarity of the copyright bit is
    unspecified).
 8. Channels 3/6/8 need TDM layout on I2S3's four lanes; the card declares
    stereo only. Untested.
 9. A modeset with a running stream: the SEC0 registers survive a video soft
    reset (15.4.7.15: the register set is unaffected) but not aux_disable (reset);
    after a hot-plug the stream must be re-prepared (audio_on is cleared).
10. I2S3 clocking: the DP core is the I2S slave; I2S3 must be bit/frame master.
    How the core derives its audio clock in asynchronous mode (BCLK, LRCK or a
    separate pin) is not stated in the UM.

Assisted-by: Claude
---

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/Kconfig
@@ -24,6 +24,15 @@ config DRM_SUN60I_DP
 	# by construction and the select needs no condition.
 	select DRM_AUX_HPD_BRIDGE
 	select GENERIC_PHY
+	# DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DP_AUDIO: drm_bridge_connector registers an hdmi-codec
+	# through the helper below. The codec itself is a platform driver found
+	# by name at run time, so it is imply, not select: this driver is
+	# built in on the product kernel and SND_SOC is a module there, and a
+	# select of a symbol that depends on SND_SOC from a =y symbol is an
+	# unmet-dependency warning (mainline's `select ... if SND_SOC` idiom has
+	# the same hole, rockchip/Kconfig:20).
+	select DRM_DISPLAY_HDMI_AUDIO_HELPER
+	imply SND_SOC_HDMI_CODEC
 	help
 	  Enable this for the DisplayPort transmitter of the Allwinner A733
 	  (sun60iw2): the AUX channel that carries DPCD and EDID, the main link
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -64,6 +64,7 @@
 #include <linux/clk.h>
 #include <linux/debugfs.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/hdmi.h>
 #include <linux/i2c.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
@@ -81,6 +82,9 @@
 #include <linux/uaccess.h>
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
 
+#include <sound/asoundef.h>
+#include <sound/hdmi-codec.h>
+
 #include <drm/bridge/aux-bridge.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h>
@@ -180,6 +184,63 @@ static const unsigned int sun60i_dp_link_rates[] = { 810000, 540000, 270000, 162
 #define DP_INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE		0x0094		/* 15.4.7.16 */
 #define  INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE_SST	BIT(0)		/* "only bit 0 is valid" */
 
+/*
+ * Audio: the SEC0 block, UM 15.4.7.65-87 (register list 15.4.6, "SEC0_AUDIO_*"
+ * at 0x0900-0x0958). Field names are the UM's own.
+ */
+#define DP_CORE_CAPABILITIES		0x00f8		/* 15.4.7, read-only */
+#define  CORE_CAP_AUDIO_PRESENT		BIT(9)		/* "AUDIO_PRESENT" */
+#define DP_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE		0x0900		/* 15.4.7.65, reset 0x2 */
+#define  SEC0_AUDIO_PACKET_ENABLE	BIT(0)
+#define  SEC0_AUDIO_MUTE		BIT(1)		/* the VBID audio-mute bit */
+#define DP_SEC0_AUDIO_INPUT_SELECT	0x0904		/* 15.4.7.66 */
+#define  SEC0_AUDIO_SRC_MASK		GENMASK(1, 0)
+#define  SEC0_AUDIO_SRC_I2S		0		/* "00 = I2S" */
+#define DP_SEC0_CHANNEL_COUNT		0x0908		/* 15.4.7.67 */
+#define  SEC0_CHANNEL_COUNT_MASK	GENMASK(3, 0)
+#define DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE	0x0910		/* 15.4.7.69, bits 6:0 */
+#define DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_RATE		0x0914		/* 15.4.7.70, bits 6:0 */
+#define DP_SEC0_AUDIO_CLOCK_MODE	0x0920		/* 15.4.7.73 */
+#define  SEC0_AUDIO_CLOCK_ASYNC		0		/* "0 ... asynchronous" */
+#define DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_SELECT	0x0934		/* 15.4.7.78 */
+#define DP_SEC0_SDP_BUFFER_DATA		0x0938		/* 15.4.7.79 */
+#define DP_SEC0_CS_SOURCE_FORMAT	0x0940		/* 15.4.7.81 */
+#define  CS_SOURCE_NUMBER		GENMASK(7, 4)
+#define  CS_LINEAR_PCM			BIT(3)		/* 0 = linear PCM */
+#define  CS_PCM_AUDIO_FORMAT		GENMASK(2, 0)	/* CS bits 5-3 */
+#define DP_SEC0_CS_CATEGORY_CODE	0x0944		/* 15.4.7.82 */
+#define DP_SEC0_CS_LENGTH_ORIG_FREQ	0x0948		/* 15.4.7.83 */
+#define  CS_SAMPLE_WORD_LENGTH		GENMASK(7, 4)	/* CS bits 32-35 */
+#define  CS_ORIG_SAMPLING_FREQ		GENMASK(3, 0)	/* CS bits 36-39 */
+#define DP_SEC0_CS_FREQ_CLOCK_ACCURACY	0x094c		/* 15.4.7.84 */
+#define  CS_SAMPLING_FREQ		GENMASK(7, 4)	/* CS bits 24-27 */
+#define  CS_CLOCK_ACCURACY		GENMASK(3, 0)	/* CS bits 28-29 */
+#define DP_SEC0_AUDIO_CHANNEL_MAP	0x0954		/* 15.4.7.86 */
+#define  SEC0_CHANNEL_MAP_IDENTITY	0x76543210	/* CHANNEL_n_MAP = n */
+#define DP_SEC0_AUDIO_FIFO_OVERFLOW	0x0958		/* 15.4.7.87, read-only */
+
+/*
+ * SEC0_CHANNEL_COUNT[3:0], UM 15.4.7.67: 0010 = 2 ch, 0011 = 2 ch + LFE,
+ * 0110 = 5.1, 1000 = 7.1. Those are the only codes the UM lists, so
+ * those are the only counts accepted: 2, 3 (2.1), 6 and 8.
+ */
+#define SEC0_CHANNELS_2			0x2
+#define SEC0_CHANNELS_2_LFE		0x3
+#define SEC0_CHANNELS_6			0x6
+#define SEC0_CHANNELS_8			0x8
+
+/*
+ * Which of the seven generic-SDP slots (SEC0_INFOFRAME_SELECT "valid values 0
+ * through 6", 15.4.7.78) carries the audio InfoFrame. The UM gives the slots no
+ * meaning, so 0 is this driver's own choice, not a documented assignment.
+ */
+#define SEC0_INFOFRAME_AUDIO		0
+
+/* DP audio InfoFrame SDP header version, HB3 bits 7:2 (mtk_dp.c:52 and
+ * msm/dp/dp_audio.c:68 both use 0x11 for DP 1.4).
+ */
+#define SUN60I_DP_SDP_VERSION		0x11
+
 /*
  * ⚠ 0x0800 and 0x0804 have NO ROW in the UM's register list: 15.4.6 runs from
  * CORE_ID at 0x00FC straight to SRC0_SECONDARY_DATA_WINDOW at 0x0808. They are
@@ -375,6 +436,11 @@ struct sun60i_dp {
 	u8			bpc;
 	bool			video_configured;
 	bool			video_on;
+	/* An audio stream has been programmed by dp_audio_prepare and not shut
+	 * down; .mute_stream only touches the core while it is true. Guarded
+	 * by dp->lock like every field here.
+	 */
+	bool			audio_on;
 	/* The KMS half. Not a pointer: devm_drm_bridge_alloc() allocates this
 	 * whole struct with the bridge embedded in it, which is why the
 	 * container_of() below is the standard shape for a bridge driver.
@@ -1328,6 +1394,10 @@ static void sun60i_dp_aux_disable(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
 	drm_dp_dpcd_set_powered(&dp->aux, false);
 
 	sun60i_dp_video_disable(dp);
+	/* The block is about to be reset, which takes the SEC0 programming with
+	 * it: an audio stream that survives this has to be prepared again.
+	 */
+	dp->audio_on = false;
 	sun60i_dp_link_release(dp);
 	/* Only the adapter this function's counterpart added. The core's one
 	 * outlives an aux_enable cycle and is removed by .detach.
@@ -2913,6 +2983,274 @@ static void sun60i_dp_bridge_detach(struct drm_bridge *bridge)
 	sun60i_dp_aux_unregister_locked(dp);
 }
 
+/*
+ * AUDIO, as a DRM_BRIDGE_OP_DP_AUDIO bridge: drm_bridge_connector registers an
+ * hdmi-codec under this device (drm_hdmi_audio_helper.c:184, parent =
+ * bridge->hdmi_audio_dev) and forwards its startup / prepare / shutdown /
+ * mute_stream here (drm_bridge_connector.c:455-549). The codec's DAI is what
+ * a simple-audio-card names with `sound-dai = <&edp0>`: the component's of_node
+ * is its parent device's (soc-core.c:827-835). The CPU side is I2S3, which the
+ * UM assigns to HDMI or eDP (ch17) and which reaches this
+ * core over an internal wire, not a pad.
+ *
+ * Mainline's own DP audio bridges are the model for the callback contract
+ * (msm/dp/dp_audio.c, mediatek/mtk_dp.c, rockchip/cdn-dp-core.c) but none of
+ * them has this register file, and zynqmp_dp_audio.c, whose core is
+ * register-similar (AUDIO_CONTROL / AUDIO_CHANNELS / M_AUD / N_AUD,
+ * zynqmp_dp.c:1383-1415), is an ASoC DAI driver with its own DMA and not a
+ * bridge, so only its shape is borrowed: channels, then clocks, then one
+ * enable write, and the enable write last.
+ *
+ * THE SEQUENCE, from the UM. 15.4.7.65: the control registers have to be
+ * programmed before the secondary-channel audio is enabled, so
+ * SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE is written last. Before: 0x0904 source = I2S, 0x0908
+ * channel count, 0x0920 clock mode, 0x0954 channel map, 0x0940-0x094c channel
+ * status, then the InfoFrame (0x0934 select, 0x0938 bytes, 0x0914 rate, 0x0910
+ * enable).
+ *
+ * CLOCK MODE. 15.4.7.73 offers asynchronous mode (0, the reset value) and
+ * 15.4.7.71/72 say MAUD/NAUD matter only when the audio clock generation is
+ * in synchronous mode. Asynchronous is therefore chosen and
+ * MAUD/NAUD are not written: no rate table, no dependency on the link rate.
+ * The vendor does the opposite -- synchronous mode with a 2.7/1.62 Gb/s table
+ * (trilinear_dp14.c:57-80, :1056-1066) -- and that table has a typo (a "90600"
+ * row whose M/N are 96 kHz's). If asynchronous mode produces no sound on
+ * silicon, the fallback needs no rebuild: DP 1.4 section 2.2.5.2.2 gives
+ * Maud/Naud = 512 * fs / link-symbol-clock reduced (48 kHz at 2.7 Gb/s is
+ * 512/5625, 44.1 kHz is 784/9375, matching the vendor's rows),
+ * `devmem 0x05740918 32 512; devmem 0x0574091c 32 5625; devmem 0x05740920 32 1`.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_audio_channel_code(unsigned int channels)
+{
+	switch (channels) {
+	case 2:
+		return SEC0_CHANNELS_2;
+	case 3:
+		return SEC0_CHANNELS_2_LFE;
+	case 6:
+		return SEC0_CHANNELS_6;
+	case 8:
+		return SEC0_CHANNELS_8;
+	default:
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Channel status, UM 15.4.7.81-84. The UM names each field by its IEC 60958-3
+ * channel-status bit numbers; the byte layout of the same bits in
+ * struct snd_aes_iec958 is include/sound/asoundef.h's, and hdmi-codec fills it
+ * from the stream parameters (snd_pcm_create_iec958_consumer_hw_params()).
+ *
+ * Not written: SEC0_CS_COPYRIGHT (0x0950) and SEC0_CS_USER_DATA (0x0960). The
+ * UM does not say whether register bit 0 is the channel-status bit 2 itself
+ * (0 = copyright asserted) or an "asserted" flag (1 = asserted), and a wrong
+ * guess flips the assertion. They stay at their reset value, 0.
+ */
+static void sun60i_dp_audio_channel_status(struct sun60i_dp *dp, const u8 *cs)
+{
+	writel(FIELD_PREP(CS_SOURCE_NUMBER, cs[2] & 0xf) |
+	       FIELD_PREP(CS_LINEAR_PCM, !!(cs[0] & IEC958_AES0_NONAUDIO)) |
+	       FIELD_PREP(CS_PCM_AUDIO_FORMAT,
+			  (cs[0] & IEC958_AES0_CON_EMPHASIS) >> 3),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_CS_SOURCE_FORMAT);
+	writel(cs[1], dp->base + DP_SEC0_CS_CATEGORY_CODE);
+	writel(FIELD_PREP(CS_SAMPLE_WORD_LENGTH, cs[4] & 0xf) |
+	       FIELD_PREP(CS_ORIG_SAMPLING_FREQ, cs[4] >> 4),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_CS_LENGTH_ORIG_FREQ);
+	writel(FIELD_PREP(CS_SAMPLING_FREQ, cs[3] & 0xf) |
+	       FIELD_PREP(CS_CLOCK_ACCURACY,
+			  (cs[3] & IEC958_AES3_CON_CLOCK) >> 4),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_CS_FREQ_CLOCK_ACCURACY);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The audio InfoFrame, through the generic-SDP mechanism of UM 15.4.7.69,
+ * .70, .78 and .79: clear the slot's enable bit, select the slot (which
+ * points the SRAM offset at the first byte of the chosen
+ * packet), stream the bytes into SDP_BUFFER_DATA, then set the rate bit
+ * (continuous, once per frame) and the enable bit.
+ *
+ * ⚠ THE UM DOES NOT SAY WHAT THE BYTES ARE. It only names the data register as
+ * the byte written into the SDP SRAM, and is silent on whether HB0-HB3 are part of
+ * what is written, or on how long a slot is. This writes what
+ * hdmi_audio_infoframe_pack_for_dp() produces -- header, then payload, 14
+ * bytes -- which is the layout every mainline DP SDP register file takes
+ * (mtk_dp_setup_sdp_aui(), msm_dp_audio_setup_sdp()), and writes no more than
+ * the packet so that a short slot is not overrun. Unproven here, owed on
+ * silicon; the vendor driver never writes an audio InfoFrame at all
+ * (trilinear_dp14.c:1091-1099), so it is no evidence either way. A sink that
+ * rejects the packet loses the speaker allocation, not the audio: the audio
+ * stream packets carry their own channel count.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_audio_infoframe(struct sun60i_dp *dp,
+				     const struct hdmi_audio_infoframe *frame)
+{
+	struct dp_sdp sdp = {};
+	ssize_t len;
+	u32 val;
+	int i;
+
+	len = hdmi_audio_infoframe_pack_for_dp(frame, &sdp, SUN60I_DP_SDP_VERSION);
+	if (len < 0)
+		return len;
+
+	val = readl(dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE);
+	writel(val & ~BIT(SEC0_INFOFRAME_AUDIO),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE);
+
+	writel(SEC0_INFOFRAME_AUDIO, dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_SELECT);
+	for (i = 0; i < len; i++)
+		writel(((const u8 *)&sdp)[i], dp->base + DP_SEC0_SDP_BUFFER_DATA);
+
+	val = readl(dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_RATE);
+	writel(val | BIT(SEC0_INFOFRAME_AUDIO),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_RATE);
+	val = readl(dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE);
+	writel(val | BIT(SEC0_INFOFRAME_AUDIO),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Audio needs a live main link: the secondary-data packets ride in the video
+ * blanking, so with no stream there is nothing to carry them, and the SEC0
+ * registers are only reachable while the block is clocked and out of reset.
+ * AUDIO_PRESENT in CORE_CAPABILITIES is the core's own answer to whether the
+ * audio half was built in.
+ */
+static int sun60i_dp_audio_check_locked(struct sun60i_dp *dp)
+{
+	lockdep_assert_held(&dp->lock);
+
+	if (!dp->enabled || !dp->video_on)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	if (!(readl(dp->base + DP_CORE_CAPABILITIES) & CORE_CAP_AUDIO_PRESENT))
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int sun60i_dp_audio_startup(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
+				   struct drm_connector *connector)
+{
+	struct sun60i_dp *dp = bridge_to_dp(bridge);
+
+	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
+
+	return sun60i_dp_audio_check_locked(dp);
+}
+
+static int sun60i_dp_audio_prepare(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
+				   struct drm_connector *connector,
+				   struct hdmi_codec_daifmt *fmt,
+				   struct hdmi_codec_params *hparms)
+{
+	struct sun60i_dp *dp = bridge_to_dp(bridge);
+	int chan, ret;
+
+	/* 15.4.7.66: "00 = I2S" is the only source this driver selects. The
+	 * UM's I2S figure (15.4.4.2) is the plain I2S shape; left- and
+	 * right-justified are not offered.
+	 */
+	if (fmt->fmt != HDMI_I2S)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	chan = sun60i_dp_audio_channel_code(hparms->channels);
+	if (chan < 0)
+		return chan;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
+
+	ret = sun60i_dp_audio_check_locked(dp);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* Packets off and muted first: it is also the only way the UM gives to
+	 * clear a FIFO overflow (15.4.7.87), and it keeps a re-prepare from
+	 * sending a half-programmed stream.
+	 */
+	writel(SEC0_AUDIO_MUTE, dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE);
+
+	writel(FIELD_PREP(SEC0_AUDIO_SRC_MASK, SEC0_AUDIO_SRC_I2S),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_INPUT_SELECT);
+	writel(FIELD_PREP(SEC0_CHANNEL_COUNT_MASK, chan),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_CHANNEL_COUNT);
+	writel(SEC0_AUDIO_CLOCK_ASYNC, dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_CLOCK_MODE);
+	/* No remap: input channel n to output channel n. The reset value 0
+	 * read literally maps every input to output 0; the vendor never writes
+	 * this register and plays stereo, so the reset state is probably a
+	 * bypass and identity is the same thing -- unproven, see item 6 of
+	 * OWED ON SILICON in the commit message.
+	 */
+	writel(SEC0_CHANNEL_MAP_IDENTITY, dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_CHANNEL_MAP);
+
+	sun60i_dp_audio_channel_status(dp, hparms->iec.status);
+
+	ret = sun60i_dp_audio_infoframe(dp, &hparms->cea);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(dp->dev, "audio InfoFrame not sent: %d\n", ret);
+
+	/* Last, and still muted: .mute_stream opens the stream once ALSA has
+	 * started the I2S side.
+	 */
+	writel(SEC0_AUDIO_PACKET_ENABLE | SEC0_AUDIO_MUTE,
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE);
+	dp->audio_on = true;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void sun60i_dp_audio_shutdown(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
+				     struct drm_connector *connector)
+{
+	struct sun60i_dp *dp = bridge_to_dp(bridge);
+	u32 val;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
+
+	dp->audio_on = false;
+
+	/* Nothing to write into a block that is already clocked off and reset;
+	 * its registers are back at their reset values.
+	 */
+	if (!dp->enabled)
+		return;
+
+	/* Reset value of 15.4.7.65: packets off, VBID mute set. */
+	writel(SEC0_AUDIO_MUTE, dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE);
+
+	val = readl(dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE);
+	writel(val & ~BIT(SEC0_INFOFRAME_AUDIO),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_INFOFRAME_ENABLE);
+}
+
+static int sun60i_dp_audio_mute_stream(struct drm_bridge *bridge,
+				       struct drm_connector *connector,
+				       bool enable, int direction)
+{
+	struct sun60i_dp *dp = bridge_to_dp(bridge);
+
+	if (direction != SNDRV_PCM_STREAM_PLAYBACK)
+		return 0;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
+
+	if (!dp->enabled || !dp->audio_on)
+		return 0;
+
+	/* AUDIO_MUTE is the VBID audio-mute bit (15.4.7.65): the sink is told
+	 * the stream is silent, and the packets keep flowing so unmuting does
+	 * not wait for the sink to re-lock.
+	 */
+	writel(SEC0_AUDIO_PACKET_ENABLE | (enable ? SEC0_AUDIO_MUTE : 0),
+	       dp->base + DP_SEC0_AUDIO_ENABLE);
+
+	return 0;
+}
